---
/**
 * /tools/ — the shelf, sold.
 *
 * WHY THIS IS NOT JUST /work/. That page's job is "open one and see what gets
 * built"; this page's job is "pick the one that is yours and find out what it
 * costs". Buying copy — what you put in, what it remembers, what it will not do,
 * what it costs — roughly doubles a card's height, and /work/ also carries eight
 * at-scale tools underneath. Two jobs, two pages.
 *
 * EVERY TOOL KEEPS ITS /work/<slug>/ ADDRESS, and that is instrument protection
 * rather than conservatism. src/scripts/track.js classifies a click as
 * `demo_open` by testing path.startsWith('/work/'), so moving the tools would
 * silently stop the conversion counting on exactly the pages that matter most,
 * with nothing erroring anywhere.
 *
 * THE COUNT IS DERIVED, never typed (D22) — shelfSlugs comes from every group in
 * the local band, so a tool added tomorrow appears here and in the count with no
 * edit to this file.
 *
 * areaServed is US rather than New Bern. The shelf is not geographically bound,
 * and splitting it against /main-street/ is what stops the two cannibalising
 * each other in search (D29).
 */
import MarketingLayout from '../layouts/MarketingLayout.astro';
import DemoCard from '../components/DemoCard.astro';
import { demos, workGroups, shelfSlugs, shelfCount } from '../data/demos';
import { startingPrice } from '../data/services';

/* THE SHELF, not merely the local band. Review Autopilot sits in a local group
   marked offShelf: it is a tool built for a local business and belongs on
   /work/, but it is not sold here. Its drafting is sample-only, so it fails
   two of the blanket claims this page makes about everything on it — that
   each runs on your own numbers, and that nothing is modelled. Filtering on
   band alone put it on the page and inside shelfCount. */
const localGroups = workGroups.filter((g) => g.band === 'local' && !g.offShelf);
const bySlug = new Map(demos.map((d) => [d.slug, d]));

const schema = [{
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Service',
  name: 'Custom browser tools for small local business',
  provider: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'AppliedIQ Solutions' },
  areaServed: { '@type': 'Country', name: 'US' },
  hasOfferCatalog: {
    '@type': 'OfferCatalog',
    name: 'Finished tools',
    itemListElement: shelfSlugs.map((slug) => {
      const d = bySlug.get(slug)!;
      return {
        '@type': 'Offer',
        itemOffered: { '@type': 'SoftwareApplication', name: d.name, description: d.seoDescription },
        url: `/work/${slug}/`,
      };
    }),
  },
}];
---
<MarketingLayout
  title="Finished tools you can drive right now"
  description={`Small, finished tools for local business — each one runs on your own numbers, in your own browser, and nothing you type leaves the page. ${startingPrice.line}`}
  image="/og/tools.png"
  schema={schema}
>
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden border-b border-line">
    <div class="bg-grid bg-grid-fade pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 -z-10 opacity-60"></div>
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 pt-16 pb-12 sm:pt-24 sm:pb-16">
      <p class="mono-label">Finished · yours to own · nothing leaves your browser</p>
      <h1 class="mt-3 max-w-3xl font-display text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-5xl">
        The tool is already built. It just needs your numbers in it.
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        Every one of these runs on the rules that belong to your business rather than to your
        whole trade — your split, your pars, your rates, your intervals. That is exactly why no
        platform has taken any of these jobs, and why they are still done on a legal pad.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        Open any of them right now. They run on made-up numbers from a made-up town until you
        put your own in, and nothing you type is sent anywhere, stored by anyone, or seen by me.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a href="/contact/?intent=quote" class="btn btn-primary">Talk about fitting one →</a>
        <a href="/check/" class="btn btn-secondary">See what the paperwork is costing you</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {localGroups.map((group) => (
    <section class="border-b border-line">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-12 sm:py-16">
        <p class="mono-label">{group.label}</p>
        <div class="mt-6 grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
          {group.slugs.map((slug) => <DemoCard demo={bySlug.get(slug)!} />)}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  ))}

  <section class="border-b border-line">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-12 sm:py-16">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
        What all {shelfCount} of them have in common
      </h2>
      <div class="mt-6 grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4">
        <div class="panel p-5">
          <p class="mono-label">The rule is yours</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
            None of them suggests a rate, a target, a par or an interval. Those are the decisions
            you are actually paid to make, and a tool that guessed at one would be inventing the
            only part that matters.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel p-5">
          <p class="mono-label">The working is printed</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
            Every figure shows the arithmetic beside it. Nothing is modelled, forecast, smoothed or
            compared against an industry average I cannot show you.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel p-5">
          <p class="mono-label">It says when it cannot answer</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
            A count that cannot be true, a pot with nobody in it, a line with no rate on it — each
            one stops the sheet with a plain sentence rather than quietly producing a
            plausible-looking number.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel p-5">
          <p class="mono-label">Nothing leaves the page</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
            No account, no login, no upload. Even a spreadsheet you drop in is read by the page
            itself. That is not a policy I could change later — it is how they are built.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-12 sm:py-16">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
        What it costs, and what happens next
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink">
        {startingPrice.line} <span class="text-ink-muted">{startingPrice.shelf}</span>
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        {startingPrice.qualifier} The first conversation is free, and it is a conversation about
        whether one of these is actually worth having — including the answer that it is not, or
        that something you already pay for does it.
      </p>
      {/* Added 2026-09-03. Somebody on this page is choosing between tools, which
          is exactly the question the assessment answers in writing. No figure
          here — the price and its credit travel together and belong on the page
          that can carry both. */}
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        Cannot tell which of these is yours? The
        {' '}<a href="/ai-fit/" class="link-sweep">AI Fit Assessment</a> is a visit and a written
        list of what is worth building, in order, with a number beside each line.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        Fitting one means it runs on your roster, your rate card, your product list and your
        words. It ends up in an account in your name, and you get a copy of the whole thing as a
        single file that opens with the internet off. Revoke my access the next morning and it
        keeps working, because there is nothing of mine left in it.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a href="/contact/?intent=quote" class="btn btn-primary">Start a conversation →</a>
        <a href="/work/" class="btn btn-secondary">See the industrial work as well</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</MarketingLayout>
